{
  "requirement": "`AgentSettings<D>` identifies the configurations a domain can use in its cache.\nTBV implements this sealed trait for `AgentConfig` and `AgentWithTools<D>`.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait AgentSettings<D: Domain>: Settings<D> { }\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.AgentSettings`.\n\n**Obligations derived from the text**\n1. `AgentSettings<D>` is a trait with the supertrait `Settings<D>`. It marks the configurations a domain can use in its cache, which `ValidationCache::with_agent` accepts as `impl AgentSettings<D>`.\n2. The trait is sealed, so only the SDK can implement it.\n3. The SDK implements it for `AgentConfig`.\n4. The SDK implements it for `AgentWithTools<D>`.\n\n**Sealing**\n- The doctest on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#trait[AgentSettings]` is a `compile_fail` block. It carries a `{test}` reference to the subject.\n- It tries to implement `AgentSettings<D>` for a third-party `ThirdPartyConfig`. That fails because the type cannot implement the private `sealed::Settings<D>` supertrait.\n- The import paths in the doctest are valid. `agent` is a `pub mod` whose file is `config.rs`, which defines the trait, and `domain::Domain` is public. So the compile failure comes from sealing and not from a bad import.\n\n**Implementation for `AgentConfig`**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[bedrock_configuration_attaches_to_a_cache_without_being_persisted]` is marked as a test of the subject.\n- It passes an `AgentConfig::Bedrock` value to `ValidationCache::with_agent`, whose parameter is `impl AgentSettings<D>`. It then asserts that `cache().agent.backend == config`.\n- It also asserts that the agent is shared between a domain and its clone, and that the saved state omits the backend settings.\n\n**Implementation for `AgentWithTools<D>`**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[registered_tools_use_the_current_context_and_ordinary_node_ids]` is marked as a test of the subject.\n- It passes `backend.with_tool(...)`, an `AgentWithTools`, to `with_agent`.\n- Its assertions show the cache uses that configuration: the tool lists sent in the requests include the registered tools, and a second domain with different tools sees a different tool list.\n\nThe `impl` blocks `impl[AgentConfig/AgentSettings<D>]` and `impl[AgentWithTools<D>/AgentSettings<D>]` have no conditionals. Both implementing types are used through the `with_agent` bound in tests, so the type system checks that both implementations exist. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
  "references": "cec8dc27c3b2606c94129fd6e5a30835b88f6c749b7486e187768c03410c8bbd",
